"A generalized Lalanne--Kreweras involution for rectangular and staircase tableaux | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/fr/abs/2501.07573)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; These can be viewed as instances of the more general symmetry of the distribution of descents over linear extensions of graded posets, for which previous proofs by Stanley and Farley were based on the theory of $P$P-partitions and the involution principle, respectively. ... This setup allows us to construct other statistic-preserving involutions on tableaux of rectangular shape, providing a simple proof of the fact that ascents and descents are equidistributed up to a shift, and proving a conjecture of Sulanke about certain statistics in the case of three rows. ... @misc{elizalde2025symmetryascentdescentdistributions, title={Symmetry of ascent and descent distributions on rectangular and staircase tableaux}, author={Sergi Elizalde}, year={2025}, eprint={2501.07573}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.07573}, }\nSubmitted 13 Jan 2025\n\n# A generalized Lalanne--Kreweras involution for rectangular and staircase tableaux\n\nImageDartmouth College\n\nSergi Elizalde\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe give direct bijective proofs of the symmetry of the distributions of the number of ascents and descents over standard Young tableaux of shape $\\lambda$λ, where $\\lambda$λ is a rectangle $(n,n,\\dots,n)$(n,n,…,n) or a truncated staircase $(n,n-1,\\dots,n-k+1)$(n,n−1,…,n−k+1). These can be viewed as instances of the more general symmetry of the distribution of descents over linear extensions of graded posets, for which previous proofs by Stanley and Farley were based on the theory of $P$P-partitions and the involution principle, respectively. In the case of two-row rectangles $(n,n)$(n,n), our bijection is equivalent to the Lalanne--Kreweras involution on Dyck paths, which bijectively proves the symmetry of the Narayana numbers. Our bijections are defined in terms of certain arrow encodings of standard Young tableaux. This setup allows us to construct other statistic-preserving involutions on tableaux of rectangular shape, providing a simple proof of the fact that ascents and descents are equidistributed up to a shift, and proving a conjecture of Sulanke about certain statistics in the case of three rows. Finally, we use our bijections to define a possible notion of rowmotion on standard Young tableaux of rectangular shape, and to give a bijective proof of the symmetry of the number of descents on canon permutations, which have been recently studied as a variation of Stirling and quasi-Stirling permutations.\n\nView PaperPDF\n\n@misc{elizalde2025symmetryascentdescentdistributions, title={Symmetry of ascent and descent distributions on rectangular and staircase tableaux}, author={Sergi Elizalde}, year={2025}, eprint={2501.07573}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.07573}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBaranyai–Katona wreath conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/366634/baranyai-katona-wreath-conjecture)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; A wreath is a set of the form $\\mathcal{F}_{\\pi}$F π​ obtained from a permutation $\\pi$π of $\\mathbb{Z}_{n}$Z n​ by taking the cyclically indexed blocks of $k$k consecutive values, as defined in the paper. ... Jan Petr and Pavel Turek, “Intervals in Dyck paths and the wreath conjecture”, arXiv:2501.07277 (2025).\n\n# Baranyai–Katona wreath conjecture\n\nAbout 1 year old · traced to 2025\n\nLet $k\\leq n$k≤n be positive integers. Write $\\mathbb{Z}_{n}$Z n​ for the integers modulo $n$n, and let $\\mathbb{Z}_{n}^{(k)}$Z n(k)​ be the set of $k$k-element subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_{n}$Z n​. A wreath is a set of the form $\\mathcal{F}_{\\pi}$F π​ obtained from a permutation $\\pi$π of $\\mathbb{Z}_{n}$Z n​ by taking the cyclically indexed blocks of $k$k consecutive values, as defined in the paper.\n\nThe wreath conjecture. For any positive integers $k\\leq n$k≤n, there is a decomposition of $\\mathbb{Z}_{n}^{(k)}$Z n(k)​ into disjoint wreaths.\n\nThis conjecture generalizes Baranyai's factorization theorem for complete uniform hypergraphs. It was posed by Baranyai and reformulated in terms of wreaths by Katona; the source does not state a resolution.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nJan Petr and Pavel Turek, “Intervals in Dyck paths and the wreath conjecture”, arXiv:2501.07277 (2025).\n\ncombinatoricshypergraph-decompositions\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJoint Symmetry of the Mesh Patterns 123 and 321 with Shading {0,1,2}² ∪ {(3,3)} | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/lv-zhang-mesh-123-321-joint-symmetry/)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Resolution source: D5/S3/Combinatorics/MeshPattern/MeshPatternS21.result 10.48550/arXiv.2501.00357 Source dossier Release 36a49830636b ... Zhang, Joint Equidistributions of Mesh Patterns 123 and 321 with Symmetric and Minus-Antipodal Shadings, arXiv:2501.00357v3, Section 2.4, Conjecture 2 (printed page 9), pair S21: the mesh patterns (123, R) and (321, R) are jointly equidistributed on Sₙ, where R = {0,1,2}² ∪ {(3,3)} as rendered in the source's diagram.\n\n# Joint Symmetry of the Mesh Patterns 123 and 321 with Shading {0,1,2}² ∪ {(3,3)}\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: proved\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Combinatorics/MeshPattern/MeshPatternS21.result10.48550/arXiv.2501.00357Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nShuzhen Lv and Philip B. Zhang, Joint Equidistributions of Mesh Patterns 123 and 321 with Symmetric and Minus-Antipodal Shadings, arXiv:2501.00357v3, Section 2.4, Conjecture 2 (printed page 9), pair S21: the mesh patterns (123, R) and (321, R) are jointly equidistributed on Sₙ, where R = {0,1,2}² ∪ {(3,3)} as rendered in the source's diagram.\n\nAn occurrence of (123, R) in π is a triple of positions with increasing values such that no other point of π lies in a shaded cell; (321, R) is the same with decreasing values. Joint equidistribution means that, for all n, k and l, the number of π ∈ Sₙ with k occurrences of (123, R) and l occurrences of (321, R) equals the number with l and k.\n  3. The involution therefore exchanges the two occurrence counts at every largest value simultaneously.\n\n## What would falsify this route\n\nThe statement would fail if some occurrence were not captured by its rectangle, if the involution changed a rectangle count or an incidence, or if it failed to be an involution.\n\n## Evidence to collect\n\nThe joint distributions are symmetric for every n ≤ 10; an independent referee implementation checked the occurrence criterion, the rectangle reformulation and the involution on all 4,037,914 permutations with n ≤ 10.\n\n## Scope assessment\n\n`theorem`; the statement is Conjecture 2 (pair S21) of arXiv:2501.00357v3 and is quantified over every n.\n\n  * Proved (formalized): the stronger simultaneous exchange of the occurrence indicators at every largest value, and hence the joint symmetry.\n  * Proved (paper argument, not formalized): the same exchange follows from Fomin growth diagrams on the Ferrers shape formed by the rectangles, with the cells outside the shape held fixed. Pair S22 follows from S21 by reverse and complement, as the source notes.\n  * Computed: the numbers of permutations avoiding both patterns are 1, 2, 4, 14, 70, 440, 3262, 27578, 260596 for n = 1, …, 9.\n  * Open: a formula for these numbers, and for the full joint distribution; no such formula is proved here.\n\n## Unverified assumptions\n\nThe literature screen is limited to the arXiv, GitHub and repository searches recorded above; the shading was read from the rendered diagram of arXiv v3.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacterize low-rank intersections for generic Toeplitz subspaces under permutations (https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/characterize-low-rank-generic-toeplitz-permutation-intersections)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The paper reduces the Toeplitz unlabeled sensing problem to understanding the rank of the concatenated matrix [V, PV] as the permutation matrix P varies. ... — Toeplitz Unlabeled Sensing  (2502.12778 - Hong et al., 18 Feb 2025) in Section 2, immediately preceding Conjecture \\ref{conj}\n\nWhile a complete answer to Question \\ref{que:rank-P} remains elusive, we pose the following conjecture, which we have verified by exhaustive computation for $n=2d$ and $d \\le 5$: For $V$ generic Toeplitz, all permutations for which $\\rank \\left[V, \\, PV \\right] < 2d$ are covered by Theorem \\ref{thm:MainTechnical}.\n\n— Toeplitz Unlabeled Sensing  (2502.12778 - Hong et al., 18 Feb 2025) in Section 2, immediately preceding Conjecture \\ref{conj}\n\n### Related Problems\n\n1.\n\nRank bound and extremal classification for primitive groups  2405.07307\n\n2.\n\nHamada conjecture on minimum p-rank of designs  2404.06513\n\n3.\n\nNonzero trace for permutation weights and degree of Tr RSK_{m,n,d}  2410.23009\n\n4.\n\nTopological characterization of permutation signatures in Berg’s pseudoinverse expansion  2407.14647\n\n5.\n\nEnumeration of maximal elements for two-loop separating varieties  2508.13865\n\n6.\n\nLog-rank conjecture for deterministic communication complexity  2401.14623\n\n7.\n\nRank formula in dimensions d ≥ 4 via proper {K_{d+2}, K_{d+2,d+2}}-covers  2508.11636\n\n8.\n\nExact rank of the deri